Let $M$ be a closed smooth manifold and $H:T^*M\to\mathbb{R}$ a $C^\infty$ Tonelli Hamiltonian (fiberwise strictly convex and superlinear), with Hamiltonian flow $\phi^H_t$ on $T^*M$. Fix the energy level $H^{-1}(1)$, assumed to be regular and to lie above Mañé's critical value, so that the Mather/Aubry theory of action-minimizing orbits and ergodic minimizing measures is non-trivial on this level. Recall that an invariant Borel probability measure $\mu$ for $\phi^H_t$ is called minimizing if it minimizes the Lagrangian action among invariant probability measures with the corresponding rotation vector, and an orbit is called locally minimizing if every finite piece of it minimizes the Lagrangian action among nearby curves with the same endpoints and time.

Question: Is it true that for a $C^\infty$-generic Tonelli Hamiltonian $H$ (in the sense of Baire category in the $C^\infty$ topology) there exists a closed connected subset $F \subset H^{-1}(1)$, invariant under $\phi^H_t$, such that:
(i) $F$ contains the supports of all ergodic minimizing measures of $H$ on $H^{-1}(1)$;
(ii) the set of orbits in $F$ that are forward and backward asymptotic to locally minimizing orbits is dense in $F$;
(iii) the restricted flow $\phi^H_t|_F$ admits a dense orbit in $F$?

